NNAAMMEE
     ppkkgg__ssiiggnn — sign binary packages for distribution

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ppkkgg__ssiiggnn [--CCvvii] [--DD _n_a_m_e[=_v_a_l_u_e]] [--jj _m_a_x_j_o_b_s] [--oo _d_i_r] --ss ssiiggnniiffyy22 --ss
              _p_r_i_v_k_e_y [--SS _s_o_u_r_c_e] [_p_k_g_-_n_a_m_e _._._.]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ppkkgg__ssiiggnn command is used to sign existing collections of binary pack‐
     ages created by pkg_create(1).

     It will sign the packages and optionally, produce a _S_H_A_2_5_6 manifest file
     in the output directory.  The options are as follows:

     --CC      Append sha256(1) checksums to _S_H_A_2_5_6 in the output directory,
             then sort it.

     --ii      Incremental mode.  Ignore packages that are already in the output
             repository.  Note that, in verbose mode, they will still show up
             as ‘Signed’ in the listing.

     --jj _m_a_x_j_o_b_s
             Sign existing packages in parallel.

     --oo _d_i_r  Specify output directory for signing packages.  Otherwise, signed
             packages are created in the current directory.

     --SS _s_o_u_r_c_e
             Source repository for packages to be signed.

     --ss ssiiggnniiffyy22 --ss _p_r_i_v_k_e_y
             Specify signature parameters for signed packages.  Option parame‐
             ters are as follows:

             ssiiggnniiffyy22  Choose signify(1) new style signatures, where the
                       gzip(1) compressed data is signed.

             _p_r_i_v_k_e_y   The path to the signer's private key.  For ssiiggnniiffyy, the
                       private key name is used to set the @@ssiiggnneerr annotation.
                       If a corresponding public key is found, the first sig‐
                       natures will be checked for key mismatches.

     --vv      Turn on verbose output, display ‘Signed output/pkg.tgz’ after
             each package is signed.

SSIIGGNNAATTUURREE DDEETTAAIILLSS
     The signature is stored within the gzip(1) comment, as plain text data,
     according to signify(1) --zzSS mode.  It contains the ed25519 signature,
     some meta-information, and SHA512/256 checksums for each 64K block of
     compressed data.

     Additionally, for further manual checking, the packing-list contains a
     complete manifest of files within the package, checksummed with sha256(1)
     and annotated with proper @@mmooddee, @@uusseerr, @@ggrroouupp annotations, so that
     pkg_add(1) will refuse to give special rights to any file which isn't
     properly annotated, and so that it will abort on installation of a file
     whose checksum does not match.

     Meta-information from signify(1) gets inserted in the packing-list during
     extraction, adding a @@ddiiggiittaall--ssiiggnnaattuurree annotation and a @@ssiiggnneerr annota‐
     tion for further manual inspection.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     cksum(1), pkg_add(1), signify(1), tar(1), package(5)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The ppkkgg__ssiiggnn command first appeared in OpenBSD 5.5.  The signature
     process was completely redesigned for OpenBSD 6.1.

